<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-comparison-of-dedekind-cuts" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Comparison of Dedekind Cuts
  </title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Our two perspectives on the set <m> \Q </m> (as equivalence classes of fractions and as rational Dedekind cuts) yield two natural orderings: the standard ordering <m> \leq </m> and the partial order of set containment. In fact, these orderings agree.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <corollary xml:id="corollary-ordering-dedekind-cuts-by-containment">
    <title>
      Ordering Dedekind cuts by containment
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The standard ordering <m> \leq </m> on the rational numbers <m> \Q </m> is the pullback of set containment <m> \subseteq </m> on the set <m> \R </m> of Dedekind cuts.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m> q_{ 1 } , q_{ 2 } \in \Q </m> be rational numbers. We note that
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            X_{ q_{ 1 } } \amp = \setmid{ p \in \Q }{ p \lt q_{ 1 } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            X_{ q_{ 2 } } \amp = \setmid{ p \in \Q }{ p \lt q_{ 2 } }
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        
        First suppose that <m> q_{ 1 } \leq q_{ 2 } </m>. If <m> p \in X_{ q_{ 1 } } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          p \lt q_{ 1 } \leq q_{ 2 }
        </md>
        
        and so <m> p \in X_{ q_{ 2 } } </m>; that is,
        
        <md>
          \iota \of{ q_{ 1 } } = X_{ q_{ 1 } } \subseteq X_{ q_{ 2 } } = \iota \of{ q_{ 2 } }
        </md>,
        
        so that <m> q_{ 1 } \subseteq_{ \iota } q_{ 2 } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Conversely, now suppose that <m> q_{ 1 } \subseteq_{ \iota } q_{ 2 } </m>. Then
        
        <md>
          X_{ q_{ 1 } } = \iota \of{ q_{ 1 } } \subseteq \iota \of{ q_{ 2 } } = X_{ q_{ 2 } }
        </md>.
        
        If <m> q_{ 1 } \gt q_{ 2 } </m>, then
        
        <md>
          q_{ 2 } \lt \frac{ q_{ 1 } + q_{ 2 } }{ 2 } \lt q_{ 1 }
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> \frac{ q_{ 1 } + q_{ 2 } }{ 2 } \in X_{ q_{ 1 } } \setminus X_{ q_{ 2 } } </m>. This would imply that <m> X_{ q_{ 1 } } \not \subseteq X_{ q_{ 2 } } </m>, and so we must have <m> q_{ 1 } \leq q_{ 2 } </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that <m> q_{ 1 } \leq q_{ 2 } </m> if and only if <m> q_{ 1 } \subseteq_{ \iota } q_{ 2 } </m>, and so <m> \leq </m> and <m> \subseteq_{ \iota } </m> are the same binary relation on <m> \Q </m>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </corollary>
  
  <p>
    <xref ref="corollary-ordering-dedekind-cuts-by-containment"/> implies that comparing rational numbers is the same as comparing their associated rational Dedekind cuts by containment. This gives us the template for how to compare Dedekind cuts in general: we compare by set containment.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="theorem-comparison-of-dedekind-cuts">
    <title>
      Comparison of Dedekind cuts
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set containment <m> \subseteq </m> is a total order on the set <m> \R </m> of Dedekind cuts.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <proof>
      <p>
        Since set containment is a partial order, it suffices to show only totality. To that end, let <m> X , Y \in \R </m> be Dedekind cuts, and suppose that <m> X \not \subseteq Y </m>. Then there is some rational number <m> q \in X </m> so that <m> q \notin Y </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Let <m> p \in Y </m>. If <m> q \leq p </m>, then we would have <m> q \in Y </m>, and so we must have <m> p \leq q </m>. But this implies that <m> p \in X </m>, and so <m> Y \subseteq X </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        In summary, we have shown that for all Dedekind cuts <m> X , Y \in \R </m>, either <m> X \subseteq Y </m> or <m> Y \subseteq X </m>. Thus set containment <m> \subseteq </m> is a total order on the set <m> \R </m> of Dedekind cuts.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  
  <p>
    Just as with the integers <m> \Z </m> and rational numbers <m> \Q </m>, we can use the standard ordering on <m> \R </m> to distinguish Dedekind cuts by their <term> sign </term>: either <term> positive </term>, <term> negative </term>, or <term> zero </term>.
  </p>
  
  <definition xml:id="definition-sign-of-a-dedekind-cut">
    <title>
      Sign of a Dedekind cut
    </title>
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall that
        
        <md>
          X_{ 0 } = \setmid{ q \in \Q }{ q \lt 0 }
        </md>
        
        is the rational Dedekind cut associated to the rational number <m> 0 </m>.
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>
              Positivity
            </title>
            
            <p>
              A Dedekind cut <m> X \in \R </m> is called <term> positive </term> if it strictly contains <m> X_{ 0 } </m>; that is, <m> X </m> is positive if <m> X \supsetneq X_{ 0 } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Negativity
            </title>
            
            <p>
              A Dedekind cut <m> X \in \R </m> is called <term> negative </term> if it is strictly contained in <m> X_{ 0 } </m>; that is, <m> X </m> is negative if <m> X \subsetneq X_{ 0 } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Non-negativity
            </title>
            
            <p>
              A Dedekind cut <m> X \in \R </m> is called <term> non-negative </term> if it contains <m> X_{ 0 } </m>; that is, <m> X </m> is non-negative if <m> X \supseteq X_{ 0 } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <title>
              Non-positivity
            </title>
            
            <p>
              A Dedekind cut <m> X \in \R </m> is called <term> non-positive </term> if it is contained in <m> X_{ 0 } </m>; that is, <m> X </m> is positive if <m> X \subseteq X_{ 0 } </m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        
        In particular, the <term> sign </term> of a Dedekind cut is either positive, negative, or zero.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      This classification of Dedekind cuts by sign will be useful in formalizing the multiplication of Dedekind cuts. We now proceed in that direction, extending the usual operations of arithmetic on <m> \Q </m> to binary operations on <m> \R </m> and determining their properties.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</subsection>
